  guideTitle="Dishwasher Detergent for Bakeries"
  title="Dishwasher Detergent for Bakery Operations — Which Type, How Much"
  description="Choosing the right commercial dishwasher detergent for bakery use. Alkaline vs enzyme, pH ranges, rinse aid selection, and brand recommendations."
>
  <h1 class="mb-4">Dishwasher Detergent for Bakery Operations</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-bakery-navy/80 mb-8">Wrong detergent in the JD-3 (or any commercial dishwasher) means either dirty output or damaged equipment. Here is the practical guide to detergent selection for bakery use.</p>

  <h2 class="mb-3">The Two Main Categories</h2>

  <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2">1. Alkaline detergents (pH 11–13)</h3>
  <p>The workhorse of commercial dishwashing. Effective on fats, oils, sugar residue, baked-on food. Works in conjunction with high wash temperature (60–65°C) to emulsify and lift residue.</p>
  <p class="mt-3"><strong>Best for bakery use.</strong> Most JD-3 customers use a general-purpose alkaline detergent at pH 12–13.</p>

  <h3 class="mt-6 mb-2">2. Enzyme detergents (pH 7–10)</h3>
  <p>Lower pH, contains proteases and amylases that break down protein and starch residues. Gentler on aluminum, hand-engraved tableware, and silver.</p>
  <p class="mt-3"><strong>Use when:</strong> Heavy egg-wash residue, dough residue, or you have aluminum or silver tableware to protect.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Recommended Brands</h2>
  <p>These are major commercial dishwasher detergent brands available globally. We do not have exclusive partnerships — use what's locally available.</p>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-3">
    <li><strong>Ecolab Apex / Solid Power XL</strong> — premium, global availability</li>
    <li><strong>Diversey Suma Crystal / Suma Star-Plus</strong> — high quality, broadly available</li>
    <li><strong>JohnsonDiversey Suma</strong> — strong in EU</li>
    <li><strong>Procter & Gamble Cascade ProSeries</strong> — North America</li>
    <li><strong>Henkel Pro-Care</strong> — strong in EU and MENA</li>
    <li><strong>Local commercial-grade alternatives</strong> often work fine; consumer dishwasher tabs do not</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Critical: Low-Foam Formulation</h2>
  <p>Commercial dishwashers (including JD-3) need <strong>low-foam</strong> detergent. Consumer-grade detergents are formulated for visible suds (psychological cue for consumer satisfaction). In a commercial machine, those suds clog the pump, reduce spray pressure, and overflow the wash tank.</p>
  <p class="mt-3"><strong>If your output is foggy or your machine is throwing error codes:</strong> first thing to check is whether you're using consumer-grade detergent. Switch to a low-foam commercial product immediately.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Rinse Aid</h2>
  <p>Rinse aid (a surfactant added to the final rinse water) reduces water surface tension so droplets sheet off cleanly. Result: no water spots on plates or glasses, faster drying.</p>
  <p class="mt-3">For bakery operations, rinse aid is optional for trays and pans (which don't show water spots) but recommended for any glassware, ceramic plates, or stainless work surfaces you wash.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Detergent Dosing</h2>
  <p>The JD-3's optional auto-detergent dispenser delivers ~3–5 mL of liquid detergent per cycle into the wash tank. For manual dosing (without the dispenser): add ~50 mL of liquid detergent to the wash tank at startup; top up after every 30–40 cycles.</p>
  <p class="mt-3">Over-dosing wastes detergent and may leave residue on items. Under-dosing means dirty output. The auto-dispenser eliminates this guesswork.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Hard Water Considerations</h2>
  <p>If your municipal water exceeds 150 ppm total hardness (typical in much of the Middle East, Mediterranean, and US Southwest), add a water softening additive or invest in the JD-3 water treatment module. Without water treatment, scale builds up on heating elements, reducing efficiency and eventually causing failure.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">What NOT to Use</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-3">
    <li>Bleach or chlorine-based products (corrode SUS304 over time)</li>
    <li>Consumer dishwasher tabs/pods (high foam, wrong chemistry)</li>
    <li>Hand-dish soap (designed for foam, not for machine washing)</li>
    <li>Vinegar in regular cycles (use only in dedicated descaling cycles)</li>
  </ul>
